      "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n**Root Cause**\nThe vulnerability stems from missing input validation in the GaugeFactoryCL.createGauge() function combined with immutable architecture in the GaugeCL contract.\n```solidity\naddress internal rHYBR;  // Initialized to address(0) by default\n   \n   function createGauge(...) external returns (address) {\n       // @audit No validation here\n       last_gauge = address(new GaugeCL(_rewardToken, rHYBR, ...));\n   }\n```\n\nThe ```rHYBR``` state variable defaults to ```address(0)``` upon contract deployment. The ```createGauge()``` function directly passes this value to the ```GaugeCL constructor``` without validating it's been initialized.\n\nImmutable Gauge Architecture\nOnce a GaugeCL is deployed, its rHYBR address is immutable (set in constructor, no setter function). This design decision, while gas-efficient, creates a permanence problem when combined with the missing validation.\n\nDeployment Sequence Dependency\n\n```solidity\n\nfunction setRHYBR(address _rHYBR) external {\n       require(owner() == msg.sender, 'not owner');\n       rHYBR = _rHYBR;\n   }\n```\nThe protocol assumes ```setRHYBR()``` will be called before any gauge creation, but enforces no such requirement. This creates a critical race condition during deployment.\n\n## The vulnerability is silent until withdrawal, making it particularly dangerous.\n\n**IMPACT**\n1. Complete and Permanent Fund Loss \n```solidity\n// In GaugeCL.sol\nfunction withdraw(uint256 tokenId) external {\n    _getReward();  //@audit ← Always called before withdrawal\n    // ... withdrawal logic\n}\n\nfunction _getReward() internal {\n    uint256 rewardAmount = earned(msg.sender);\n    if (rewardAmount > 0) {\n        rewardToken.safeApprove(rHYBR, rewardAmount);\n        IRHYBR(rHYBR).depostionEmissionsToken(rewardAmount);  //@audir ← Reverts on address(0)\n        IRHYBR(rHYBR).redeemFor(rewardAmount, 0, msg.sender); //@audit ← Never reached\n    }\n}\n```\nWhen rHYBR == address(0) and rewards exist:\n\nwithdraw() always calls _getReward()\n_getReward() attempts to call functions on address(0)\nEVM reverts on all calls to address(0) (proven in test)\nWithdrawal transaction fails completely\nNFT remains locked forever\n\n2.  No Recovery Mechanism\n```solidity\n//@audit Calling this AFTER gauge creation does nothing\ngaugeFactory.setRHYBR(correctAddress);\ngauge.rHYBR(); // Still returns address(0)\n``` \nOnly updates factory storage, doesn't affect already-deployed gauges. \nThe Emergency Mode Insufficient\n```solidity\nfunction activateEmergencyMode() external onlyOwner {\n    emergency = true;  // Only blocks NEW operations\n}\n```\n3. Protocol Reputation Damage \n   Immediate Loss of Trust: Users lose life savings in \"unrecoverable\" contract\n   Legal Liability: Potential lawsuits for negligence\n   Competitor Advantage: Other protocols capitalize on the failure\n   Media Coverage: Negative press coverage in crypto media\n\n4. Systemic DeFi Risk \n   If the broken gauge is whitelisted in other protocols:\n   a. Composability breaks (gauge tokens become worthless)\n   b. Downstream protocols may integrate broken gauges\n\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nAdd Validation in createGauge()",
